course: dictionary
lesson: 8245
title: Rhetorical questions
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A rhetorical question is a question you ask to make a point, not to get an answer. «Who wants to live in a world without trees?» Nobody answers that, because the writer already knows the answer is nobody. The question makes you think and agree.
explain: Writers use rhetorical questions in speeches and persuasive writing to make readers feel something. «Isn't it time we did something?» wants you to agree that it is time. Look for them alongside other persuasive tricks, such as reasons given in order and repeated words.
explain: Rhetorical also describes the art of speaking or writing in a way that persuades or impresses people. Formal writers call this rhetoric. Be careful, though: rhetoric can be fancy speech that hides a lack of real action, so check whether the words are backed up by deeds.
fact: A rhetorical question is asked for effect, not to get an answer.
fact: «Surely you agree?» is a rhetorical question that makes the reader feel they should agree.
fact: «Do you know what time it is?» said by a cross parent is rhetorical: no answer is needed.
fact: Rhetorical also means to do with the art of persuasive speaking or writing, and «rhetorical device» is a technique used to persuade.
fact: «Empty rhetoric» means fine-sounding words that are not backed up by action.
exercise: Which is a rhetorical question? | «Where is the station?» asked by a lost tourist | «How many apples are in the bag?» asked to count them | «What time is it?» asked by a friend who wants to know | *«Who wants to live in a world without trees?»
exercise: Paul's dad says, «Do you know what time it is?» in a cross voice. What is he doing? | Singing a song | Asking so he can set his watch | Telling the time by the clock | *Making a point, with no answer needed
exercise: The speaker says, «Isn't it time we did something?» What does she want the audience to do? | *Agree that action is needed | Leave the hall quietly | Count the chairs | Argue with her about the weather
test: Why does a writer use a rhetorical question in a speech? | To test the microphone | To get the audience to answer out loud | To make the speech longer | *To make listeners think and agree with the point
test: What does «empty rhetoric» suggest? | A long list of numbers | *Fancy words that may hide a lack of real action | A loud whisper | Very clear facts with proof
exam: Which of these is a rhetorical question? | What is the capital of France? | *Surely you agree? | Could you pass the salt, please? | Where did you leave your coat?
